    {
      "id": "SPF-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "anchor": "inputs-progression-finite-difference",
      "title": "The scaled endpoint difference restores the prime sum and isolates the exceptional character",
      "statement": "At u=(1+δ)y, scaling by yu/(u−y) recovers ψχ(y) up to the short interval. The sharp error is at most 4yE/δ+20δy Σ aρ y^(Re ρ−1)+4(δy+1)log(2y). With T=m^12 and δ=m^−5 the total character error in the large-modulus range is at most By/m²+22yW. Character orthogonality gives the individual progression main terms.",
      "hypotheses": "y≥2, 0<δ≤1/2, one finite list and one constant at both arguments, 1/2≤Re ρ<1, and E bounds the smoothed error at both endpoints. The final specialization uses 8|m and the two displayed large-modulus range inequalities. The class a is relatively prime to m.",
      "definitions": [
        "W is the sum over all characters of the residual zero weights with one selected exceptional occurrence removed if listed."
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "SPF-01"
      ],
